WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.602
You ask an assistant for a Kubernetes Deployment for hello-devops.

2
00:00:07.602 --> 00:00:09.235
No files, no versions.

3
00:00:09.235 --> 00:00:10.869
It answers in seconds.

4
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:15.461
A model writes by predicting likely text, one small piece at a time.

5
00:00:15.461 --> 00:00:17.385
It is not looking anything up.

6
00:00:17.385 --> 00:00:20.784
It produces the shape it has seen thousands of times.

7
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.822
Common shapes come out well.

8
00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:27.636
Rare details come out as guesses, and the guesses look exactly like facts.

9
00:00:27.636 --> 00:00:28.807
Can you spot them?

10
00:00:29.033 --> 00:00:31.197
So one rule for this whole lesson.

11
00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:34.188
The model drafts, and a tool you trust decides.

12
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.436
Every draft goes round a loop.

13
00:00:36.436 --> 00:00:36.979
Read it.

14
00:00:36.979 --> 00:00:38.471
Run a check you trust.

15
00:00:38.471 --> 00:00:42.068
Fix what it finds, and run it again, until it passes.

16
00:00:42.068 --> 00:00:45.121
Then review the whole diff as a pull request.

17
00:00:45.333 --> 00:00:46.556
First, kubeconform.

18
00:00:46.556 --> 00:00:51.832
It checks each object against the official schema for its kind, without a cluster.

19
00:00:52.033 --> 00:00:55.977
It finds two mistakes that a YAML parser would never notice.

20
00:00:55.977 --> 00:00:58.935
containerPort must be a number, not a string.

21
00:00:58.935 --> 00:01:01.564
And timeout is not a probe field at all.

22
00:01:01.564 --> 00:01:03.601
The real one is timeoutSeconds.

23
00:01:03.833 --> 00:01:07.127
That second one is the mistake models make most often.

24
00:01:07.127 --> 00:01:11.519
A field that sounds right, in the right place, with perfect indentation.

25
00:01:11.519 --> 00:01:13.837
Without dash strict, it slips through.

26
00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:16.803
Fix those two lines, and run it again.

27
00:01:16.803 --> 00:01:18.603
Now kubeconform is happy.

28
00:01:18.833 --> 00:01:20.404
The API server is not.

29
00:01:20.404 --> 00:01:22.902
Some rules live only in the server.

30
00:01:22.902 --> 00:01:29.541
A server dry run sends the object, runs every check of a real apply, and then throws it away.

31
00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:31.566
It finds the third mistake.

32
00:01:31.566 --> 00:01:36.166
The selector says app hello-devops, but the Pod labels say app hello.

33
00:01:36.166 --> 00:01:37.232
They must match.

34
00:01:37.433 --> 00:01:39.467
Fix it, and both checks pass.

35
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:41.900
But is it right for hello-devops?

36
00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:43.966
Checks prove shape, not intent.

37
00:01:43.966 --> 00:01:48.765
No schema knows that your app listens on 8000, or which path is healthy.

38
00:01:48.967 --> 00:01:50.715
Read the routes in app.py.

39
00:01:50.715 --> 00:01:54.548
The probe uses slash health, but the app serves /healthz.

40
00:01:54.767 --> 00:01:57.759
Three more things are wrong for hello-devops.

41
00:01:57.759 --> 00:01:59.089
The image is latest.

42
00:01:59.089 --> 00:02:03.012
REDIS_HOST is localhost, instead of the Service name redis.

43
00:02:03.012 --> 00:02:05.871
And there is no readiness probe on /readyz.

44
00:02:05.871 --> 00:02:10.459
The draft also sets a CPU limit, which the lab leaves out on purpose.

45
00:02:10.667 --> 00:02:16.455
Paste the tool output and your notes back, ask for a fixed version, and run the same checks again.

46
00:02:16.667 --> 00:02:23.415
When it matches the tested deployment file, and both checks pass, only then does it go into a pull request.

47
00:02:23.633 --> 00:02:25.207
That is the verify loop.

48
00:02:25.207 --> 00:02:30.452
A tool you trust checks every draft, and a person reviews what tools cannot see.
